# Mathematical Bibliography and Citation Style References

This shared reference summarizes AMS, EMS and LMS publishing guidance for reference lists and citation display.
Use it when choosing or checking labels, author-name presentation, sorting and bibliographic entry structure.
Actual defaults and exceptions belong to the responsible author or manuscript under the [four writing layers](../../framework/rules/preferences.md#scope-and-adoption).
The guidance below describes the identified sources; it does not select one style for every manuscript.

<a id="ams"></a>
## AMS — Labels and Bibliography Preparation

Source: [AMS Author Handbook, Journal Classes, September 2017, Chapter 2, Sections 7.2 and 7.4, pp.18–20](https://mirrors.mit.edu/CTAN/info/amscls-doc/Author_Handbook_Journals.pdf#page=18).

- Section 7.2 provides numeric `amsplain` labels, preferred for articles, and `amsalpha` labels formed from authors and publication year.
- Author-alphabetical order is usual, but style processing can alter it.
- Section 7.4 accepts initials with surnames and distinguishes printed labels from internal citation keys.

Alphabetic author–year labels are distinct from a full author–date citation scheme such as parenthesized surnames and year.
The `amsalpha` option identifies an established label scheme; additional requirements for author-name display and exact sorting need a compatible style.

<a id="ems"></a>
## EMS — Entry Structure and Reference-List Contents

Source: [EMS Press, Elemente der Mathematik, Format of manuscripts](https://ems.press/journals/em/submit).

- Journal entries give authors, title, Mathematical Reviews journal abbreviation, volume, parenthesized year and pages, in that order.
- Book entries also identify the publisher.
- References are alphabetized and restricted to works cited in the text; BibTeX is recommended.

This passage does not specify a label scheme or a mandatory author-name abbreviation.

<a id="lms"></a>
## LMS — Names, Sorting and Citation Locators

Source: [London Mathematical Society house style, Section 6.17, pp.13–16](https://www.lms.ac.uk/sites/lms.ac.uk/files/Publications/LMSHouseStyle.pdf#page=13).

- Section 6.17.1 sorts by authors and then date, numbers the ordered list, and normally uses initials with surnames while respecting author forms and accents.
- Sections 6.17.2–6.17.3 give formatting rules and examples, including standard journal abbreviations.
- Section 6.17.5 illustrates citations with theorem, section or page locators.

Its small capitals, quotation marks and other house details are separate choices, not consequences of using initials.
The source is the linked house-style document, not a statement that every LMS journal currently imposes all its provisions.

## Combining the Guidance

A policy can select the AMS alphabetic-label model, EMS entry structure and LMS name presentation independently.
State the selected defaults at their responsible scope instead of treating the three sources as one undifferentiated rule.
Check the generated bibliography's labels, author sorting and name display separately; a style name alone does not establish that all three selections are implemented.

Keep complete available author information and other verified publication facts in BibTeX.
Use the selected style to generate initials and labels while retaining family names, name particles, accents and necessary distinctions in the data.
The [canonical bibliography and manuscript-subset rules](../../framework/rules/writing.md#citations-notation-and-versions) govern data ownership and synchronization.
Retain the actual edition and article identifier when needed to identify the cited work.

Mathematical reliance, first-use citation and attribution follow the applicable [source and attribution rules](../../framework/rules/writing.md#definition-citations).
Bibliography formatting does not establish that a source supports the mathematical assertion attributed to it.
